.TH EMAIL\-TUI 1 "" "email-cli" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
email\-tui \- full-screen terminal mail reader for email-cli
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B email\-tui
.br
.B email\-tui
.RB { compose | reply | send | embed }
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Interactive reader for the accounts configured with
.BR email\-cli (1).
Started without arguments it opens the account list, then a folder or label
browser, then the message list.
.PP
It requires a terminal. For scripting use
.BR email\-cli (1)
or
.BR email\-cli\-ro (1).
.SH NAVIGATION
Arrow keys or
.BR j / k
move down and up in every list and scroll the reader,
.B Enter
opens,
.BR Backspace ", " Esc " and " q
go back one level,
.B Esc
on the account list (the top level) quits,
.B Ctrl\-C
quits at once from anywhere, and
.B ?
shows the shortcut list of the current view.
.B q
never quits the program and
.B h
is not a help key.  The help overlay and the key handler are made from one
table, so the help lists exactly the keys the view has.
.PP
Every entry of the \fB:\fR action menu has a quick key, a letter drawn at
its left; the help shows it as \fB:x\fR.  Serious entries (Delete, Send, Start
a sync) and rare ones (Move, labels, Create, Save all attachments, Renew the
authorization) ask first, with No as the default.  The letters are listed in
docs/spec/tui-keys.md.
.PP
The browser starts on the
.B Unread
virtual label; the folder list also offers
.BR Flagged ", " Junk ", " Phishing ", " Answered " and " Forwarded .
.B /
starts a content search in the current view.
.SH KEYS
.SS Message list
The help overlay (\fB?\fR) of each view opens with the same description:
.br
Message list: The messages of one folder, newest first. The cursor is on one
message. Enter reads it; space marks several to act on together.
.br
Gmail Unread, Flagged or a search result: The messages of a Gmail view that is
no label (Unread, Flagged or a search result). The cursor is on one message;
Enter reads it.
.br
Gmail Archive: The archived messages of the Gmail account: they have no label.
The cursor is on one message; Enter reads it.
.br
Gmail Trash: The messages in the Gmail Trash. The cursor is on one message;
Enter reads it, the - menu restores it to the inbox.
.PP
.TP
.BR j " or " Down ", " k " or " Up
Move the cursor.
.TP
.BR PgDn ", " PgUp ", " Home " or " g ", " End " or " G
Page down, page up, first message, last message.
.TP
.BR Esc ", " q ", " Backspace ", " Left ", " h
Back to the folders or labels; Esc, q, Left and h drop the marks, then the
filter, first.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the message.
.TP
.B /
Filter the list; Tab changes the scope, Esc clears the filter.
.TP
.B Space
Mark the message and step down;
.B x
clears the marks.  The keys that change a message act on the marked
messages, or on the one under the cursor when none is marked.
.TP
.BR c ", " r ", " A ", " F
Compose, reply, reply to all, forward.
.TP
.BR U ", " l
Refresh after a sync, open the rules overview (see RULES below).
.TP
.B *
Star or unstar (toggle the flag), in the list and on the server at once.
.TP
.B +
Toggle read and unread, at once.
.TP
.B !
Toggle junk, at once.
.TP
.BR d " or " Delete
Delete: move to Trash, or delete for good when the message is in Trash.
Asks first, naming the message by sender and subject; the default answer is
No.
.TP
.B H
The recent changes: who changed what and when, with a Revert entry in the
.B :
menu and on
.BR Enter ,
which asks first (default No).  A step that cannot be reverted (a sent mail, a
permanent delete) is marked
.BR "not revertable" .
In the reader,
.B H
shows the history of that message.
.TP
.B R
In the reader: which rules match this message and what each would do, and, after
an apply, which rule gave each of its labels (see RULES).
.TP
.B t
Gmail only: pick the labels of the message.
.TP
.B :
Open the action menu: every action that changes state, with its direct key
where it has one.  The menu also holds what has no key: start a background
sync (asks, naming the account and folder), move to a folder or label, toggle
Done (IMAP), and for Gmail archive, restore from Trash, remove the current
label, add or remove a named label.  The menu lists only what applies in the
view: restore in the Trash only, archive in every view but the Archive,
remove the current label in the view of a label only.
.TP
.B ?
Help for the view.
.SS Message reader
Description: One message, rendered as text. The keys scroll it, search in it and
reply to it; q goes back to the list.
.PP
.TP
.BR j " or " Down ", " k " or " Up ", " PgDn ", " PgUp ", " Home ", " End
Scroll.
.TP
.BR r ", " F
Reply, forward.
.TP
.B v
Toggle rendered text and raw source.
.TP
.BR / ", " n ", " N
Search the body, next and previous match.
.TP
.B a
Save an attachment.
.TP
.BR * ", " + ", " \- ", " ! ", " d " or " Delete ", " t
As in the list, on the open message.
.TP
.B :
The action menu: toggle Done (IMAP), Gmail archive and remove the current
label, and
.B Save all attachments.
.SS Label browser (Gmail)
Description (a label of your own): The labels of the Gmail account. The cursor
is on one of your own labels; Enter opens its messages, the - menu can delete
it.
.br
Description (a system label): The labels of the Gmail account. The cursor is on
a system label, which cannot be deleted; Enter opens its messages.
.PP
.TP
.BR j " or " Down ", " k " or " Up ", " PgDn ", " PgUp ", " Home " or " g ", " End " or " G
Move.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the label.
.TP
.BR Esc ", " q ", " Backspace ", " Left ", " h
Back to the accounts.
.TP
.B /
Search across folders.  The prompt takes text; Tab changes the scope, Enter
searches, Esc cancels and ctrl\-C quits.
.TP
.B :
The action menu: create a label, delete the label under the cursor (only a
label of your own; it asks, naming the label; the default answer is No).
Esc, q and Backspace close the menu and the question.
.TP
.B t
In a Gmail list or the reader: the label picker.  j and k move, Space ticks,
Enter applies, Esc, q and Backspace close it and change nothing, ctrl\-C quits.
.SS Accounts and folders
Accounts: The accounts of the program. The cursor is on one account; Enter
opens it, n adds a new one.
.br
Folders: The folders of the IMAP account, as a tree or one level at a time. The
cursor is on one folder; Enter opens its messages.
.br
Attachment picker: The attachments of the message. The cursor is on one; Enter
chooses it and asks where to save it.
.br
Rules: The mail rules that sort incoming mail, in the order they run. The cursor
is on one rule; Enter edits it, a adds a new one.
.br
Rule: One mail rule as a form: its name, the conditions as rows and as text, the
actions and a live preview of the cached messages it matches. Enter edits the
row, s saves the rule.
.PP
.TP
.BR j " or " Down ", " k " or " Up ", " PgDn ", " PgUp ", " Home ", " End
Move.
.TP
.B Enter
Open.
.TP
.B Backspace
On the account list (the top level) it does nothing and does not quit; only
.B Esc
quits there.  Everywhere else, outside a text field, it goes back like
.B Esc
and
.BR q ;
in a field it deletes.
.TP
.BR n ", " d ", " i ", " e
Accounts: add, delete (asks), edit IMAP, edit SMTP.
On a Gmail account the
.B :
menu offers "Renew the authorization...": the browser, the address only (for an SSH forward) or a pasted redirect address, as
.B email-cli config reauth
does.
.TP
.BR t ", " c ", " /
Folders: tree or flat view, compose, search.  The search prompt takes text;
Tab changes the scope, Enter searches, Esc cancels and ctrl\-C quits.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
Folders: first row, last row (as Home and End).
.TP
.BR Esc ", " q ", " Backspace ", " Left ", " h
Rules overview and rule form: back (g and G are Home and End in the overview);
the form asks first when something changed.  ctrl\-C quits at once, also at
the questions.
.TP
.BR h " or " Left ", " l " or " Right
Folders, flat view: go up one level (as Esc) and open the row (as Enter).
.TP
.B Esc ", " q ", " Backspace
Folders: up one level in the flat view, otherwise back to the accounts.
.SS Compose and review
Review: The composed message, ready to send. Check the headers and the body; e,
b and a change them, the - menu sends the message.
.br
Body editor: The body of the message being composed, as plain text. Esc saves
it and goes on to the review.
.PP
.TP
.B Esc
In the compose dialog: asks before it throws away what was typed (the default
answer is No).
.B Ctrl\-C
leaves at once.
.TP
.B :
On the review screen: send the message (asks, naming the recipients; the
default answer is No) or remove an attachment (asks which when there are
several).
.TP
.BR e ", " b ", " a ", " ? ", " q
Review screen (? is the help): edit the headers, edit the body, add an attachment (the file browser opens at once, in the directory of the last
attachment; its typing field takes a path, with ~ and Tab), discard
the draft (asks).
.SH RULES
.B l
on the message list opens the rules overview: every rule in the order the rules
run, with its name, what it does in words (the condition, then
.BR +label ", " \-label ", move to, forward to" ),
whether it is on, the number of cached messages it matches and when an apply
last changed something by it.
.TP
.BR a ", " Enter " or " e
Add a rule, edit the rule: the rule form.  The form has the name, the conditions
as rows (join with and or or, field from, to, subject, label, body or age,
operator is, contains or matches a glob, not), the same condition as text
(editable; both stay equal), the actions (any number of labels to add and to
remove, a folder, an address) picked from a list, and a live preview: how many
cached messages match and the first of them.
.B s
saves.
.TP
.BR d ", " c ", " Space ", " J " and " K
Delete (asks first), copy, switch on or off, move down or up.
.TP
.BR p " or " A
The dry run of the whole set in a scrollable report; nothing changes until
.B y
and a confirmation.  After an apply the undo of it is offered with the number of
changes;
.B u
undoes the last apply later.
.PP
The same operations are in
.BR email\-cli (1):
.BR "rules list" ", " "rules test" ", " "rules add" ", " "rules edit" ", "
.BR "rules copy" ", " "rules move" ", " "rules apply" " and " "rules revert" .
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B compose
Open the compose dialog directly.
.TP
.B reply
Reply to a message.
.TP
.B send
Send a message non\-interactively.
.TP
.BI "embed show " uid " \-\-account " address " [\-\-folder " name "] [\-\-json]"
For use inside another program: start in the message view of one message
(the account selector and the lists are skipped) and end with it.
The uid is the one printed by
.B "email\-cli list \-\-json".
The folder defaults to the one holding the uid, else INBOX.
Backspace and q leave the view and the program (exit status 0), Esc cancels
(2); an unknown account, folder or uid prints an error and exits with 1,
changing nothing on the server.
With \fB\-\-json\fP one line
.B {"viewed":true,"uid":"..","folder":"..","cancelled":false}
is written to the standard output when the view is left.
Example, from a todo page:
.B "email\-tui embed show 4711 \-\-account me@example.org \-\-json"
.TP
.BI "embed reply " uid " [\-\-all] \-\-account " address " [\-\-folder " name "] [\-\-json]"
Start in the reply editor of one message, as
.B reply
does (\fB\-\-all\fP: reply to all), and end with the send (exit status 0).
Esc, or discarding in the review screen, cancels (2) and sends nothing; a send
error is shown, the draft is kept, and the exit status is 1.
With \fB\-\-json\fP one line
.B {"sent":true,"uid":"..","folder":"..","id":"<message\-id>","cancelled":false}
is written to the standard output.
.TP
.BI "embed compose \-\-account " address " [\-\-to " addr "] [\-\-subject " text "] [\-\-body\-file " file "] [\-\-json]"
Start in the new-message editor with the recipient, the subject and the start of
the body (read from the file) filled in, and end with the send (exit status 0).
Esc, or discarding in the review screen, cancels (2) and sends nothing; a send
error is shown, the draft is kept, and the exit status is 1.
With \fB\-\-json\fP one line
.B {"sent":true,"id":"<message\-id>","cancelled":false}
is written to the standard output.
.SH FILES
The protocol traffic log (credentials masked) is written only with \fB\-\-debug\fP.
As in
.BR email\-cli\-ro (1),
plus
.I ~/.local/share/email\-cli/ui.ini
which remembers the last account and folder.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR email\-cli (1),
.BR email\-cli\-ro (1),
.BR email\-sync (1)
